  "live_records": {
    "q-334774": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334774",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=6y-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.$\\,$\nTerm ${y}^{′}$ in the second equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${y}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6y-2xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the $\\text{prey}.$ Indeed, \n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $6y$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-2xy$ term).",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
