    "q-335003": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335003",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=7x-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7x-4xy=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4xy & =0 \\\\ x(7-4y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{7}{4}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{7}{4},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}(-6+3x) & =0 \\\\ -6+3x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(2,\\frac{7}{4}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
