    "q-334992": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334992",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy & =0 \\\\ x(3-2y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ -4+5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
