    "q-335000": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335000",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-5x+4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=9y-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+4xy=0 \\\\ 9y-2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+4xy & =0 \\\\ x(-5+4y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{5}{4}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-2(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{5}{4},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-2xy & =0 \\\\ y(9-2x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}(9-2x) & =0 \\\\ 9-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
