    "q-335012": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335012",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=0.8x-0.4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-0.3y+1.2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.25,\\,2]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is\n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0.8x-0.4xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-0.3y+1.2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0.8x-0.4xy)=0.8-0.4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0.8x-0.4xy)=-0.4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.3y+1.2xy)=1.2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.3y+1.2xy)=-0.3+1.2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}0.8-0.4y & -0.4x \\\\ 1.2y & -0.3+1.2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}0.8-0.4(2) & -0.4(0.25) \\\\ 1.2(2) & -0.3+1.2(0.25)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -0.1 \\\\ 2.4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -0.1 \\\\ 2.4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-2.4\\cdot (-0.1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source).",
